Consider the Minimal Supersymmetric Standard Model (MSSM). After soft supersymmetry breaking, each Standard Model quark and lepton has a scalar superpartner (squark or slepton), and gauginos couple a fermion to its scalar partner via interactions of the form ψ̄ λ Ũ† U ψ̃, where U and Ũ are the unitary matrices that diagonalize the fermion and sfermion mass matrices respectively. Since U†Ũ need not be the identity, gaugino exchange in general induces flavor-changing neutral currents (FCNC) at rates that, for generic soft masses of order the electroweak scale, vastly exceed experimental bounds (e.g. on K0–K̄0 mixing, μ→eγ, b→sγ).

Question: Identify the physical principle, symmetry, or mediation mechanism implemented in nature that suppresses these supersymmetric FCNC contributions to acceptable levels. In particular, determine which of the following (or which combination, or which alternative) is realized:

(a) Universal scalar degeneracy producing a super-GIM cancellation (e.g. exact or approximate flavor universality of soft squark/slepton mass matrices at some high scale, as in minimal supergravity);
(b) Alignment of the fermion and sfermion mass bases (so that U†Ũ ≈ 1 in the relevant sectors, achieved by flavor symmetries acting on both fermions and scalars);
(c) Decoupling, in which the first two generations of squarks and sleptons are made sufficiently heavy (multi-TeV) to suppress their FCNC contributions while the third generation remains light;
(d) Gauge mediation of supersymmetry breaking, in which flavor-blind Standard Model gauge interactions communicate SUSY breaking to the visible sector and automatically yield degenerate sfermion masses within each gauge representation;
(e) Some other mechanism (e.g. anomaly mediation, gaugino mediation, flavor mediation, or a hybrid).

Provide the correct answer as realized in nature, together with the experimental signatures that would distinguish among (a)–(e).

